# LugalOS Lisp Dialect, Primitives & Scripting

Documentation > Developer Manual > Lisp Dialect, Primitives & Scripting

LugalOS features a self-contained Scheme-like Lisp machine (user/lisp/lisp.c) that serves as the interactive system console, an appliance scripting language, and the mathematical runtime for the Computer Algebra System (CAS).

## 1. Dialect Overview & Special Forms

The dialect is lexically scoped, dynamically typed, and evaluates expressions in applicative order.

### Special Forms
* quote / ': Inhibits evaluation.
* quasiquote / `  `, unquote / ,, unquote-splicing / ,@`: Template construction.
* lambda: Function definition with lexical closures (lambda (x y) (+ x y)).
* define: Global binding (define name val) and shorthand (define (fn args...) body...).
* set!: Mutates existing binding.
* if: Conditional branching (if test then else).
* cond: Multi-branch conditional (cond ((= x 1) 'one) (else 'other)).
* let / let*: Local bindings.
* Named let: Tail-recursive loop construct:
```lisp
  (let loop ((i 0) (sum 0))
    (if (> i 10)
        sum
        (loop (+ i 1) (+ sum i))))
```
(Note: letrec is not built-in; use named let for local recursion).
* while: Imperative loop construct (while test expr...).
* and / or: Short-circuiting logical operations.
* define-macro: Syntactic macro expansion.

## 2. Zero-SRAM Builtins Architecture

To support hundreds of primitives without exhausting the 520 KB SRAM limit on RP2350 or ESP32-C6:
* Storage in Flash .rodata: All core C primitives are compiled into static tables in user/lisp/builtins_table.h.
* Binary Search: When resolving an identifier, the evaluator first checks globalenv. If absent, it queries builtinstable using O(\log N) bsearch().
* Zero Overhead: Primitives consume 0 bytes of .bss SRAM and 0 cons pairs in global_env.

## 3. Standard Library & Modular Packages

LugalOS uses a two-tier library model:
1. Boot Library (/sd0/system/etc/stdlib.lisp): A minimal set of essential combinators (not, print, when, unless, identity) loaded at boot.
2. Modular Packages (/sd0/system/lib/): Loaded on demand via (require 'package):
* (require 'seq): Sequence utilities (map, filter, foldl, foldr, zip).
* (require 'str): String manipulation (string-split, string-join, trim).
* (require 'dict): Key-value association dictionaries and hash tables.
* (require 'matrix): Vector and matrix linear algebra routines.
* (require 'chart): Real-time graphical charting on the ribbon canvas.
* (require 'ota): The node's update state as data, read from /dev/ota. (ota-info) returns an association list of ("key" . "value") strings (board, layout, version, running, trial, slota, slotb, ata, atb, ... ; see updates); (ota-get "running") returns one value or #f. Read-only.
3. Board identity: (board) returns the board file's id (esp32p4-nano); (board-info) returns the one-line description the console banner and the boot log use: PRESET vVERSION (HARDWARE, CHIP) "NAME" uid UID. The same facts, one per line, are /proc/board.

## 4. Hardware & System Primitives

Lisp scripts can interact directly with system services and microcontroller hardware:

### Hardware I/O & Timers
* (sleep-ms ): Cooperative sleep without spinning the CPU.
* (time-ms): System uptime in milliseconds.
* (i2c-read   ): Read N bytes from I2C bus address and register.
* (i2c-write   ): Write bytes to I2C peripheral.
* (gpio-set  ): Set GPIO logic level (0 or 1).
* (gpio-get ): Read GPIO logic level.

### Sensors
Readings come from the sensor hub's cache, so no call waits on the I2C bus. Values are integers in each channel's fixed-point unit (sensors).
* (sensor-list): The detected devices, as symbols ((bme280 ina226)).
* (sensor-read): Everything, as ((hw (dev (chan . val) ...) ...) (inferred ...) (fused ...)).
* (sensor-read 'dev): One device's channels; (sensor-read 'ina226 'voltage) one value; add 'age for its age in seconds.
* (sensor-read 'chan): The best source of a channel ('temp, 'co2, ...).
* Channel names: temp, pressure, humidity, lux, eco2, tvoc, gas-res, co, no2, nh3, co2, pressure-msl, abs-humidity, dew-point, iaq, voltage (mV), current (mA), power (mW), battery (0.1 %), accel-x/y/z (mg), gyro-x/y/z (0.1 deg/s).
* (sensor-cal ['save | 'restore | 'clear]): Gas-sensor calibration baselines.

### Filesystem & Process Control
* (load ): Load and evaluate Lisp source file.
* (read-file ): Read file content directly into a Lisp string.
* (write-file  ): Write string content to disk.
* (cp  ), (mv  ), (rm ), (mkdir ), (rmdir ): Copy, rename or move (within one volume; #f if ` exists), remove; #t or #f`.
* (spawn ): Launch an isolated U-mode program in the background.
* (chess [threads]): Launch the interactive chess engine in split-terminal keyboard mode (optional multicore Lazy SMP search).
* (chess-touch): Launch the touch-first graphical chess game with interactive piece movement and Classic Mac toolbar controls.

### Windows of Their Own: ui- (ugui for Lisp)
On every board with a panel or an HDMI screen (and QEMU's RAM screen), a Lisp program can open a ribbon window and fill it with the system's own controls, the ones the launcher and the reader use. It works by touch, by mouse and by keys alone; see /sd0/demos/hellougui.lisp ((load "/sd0/demos/hellougui.lisp"), then (hello-ugui)).

The model is immediate: the program describes its whole picture in one pass and keeps its own state; nothing calls back into it.

```lisp
(ui-open "Counter")
(define n 0)
(define (draw)
  (ui-begin)
  (ui-label 16 12 (string-append "Count: " (number->string n)) #t)
  (ui-button 1 "Add one" 16 40 120 28)
  (ui-end))
(draw)
(let loop ((ev (ui-event)))
  (cond ((null? ev) (ui-close))                       ; Ctrl-C
        ((eq? (car ev) 'close) #t)                    ; the close box
        ((eq? (ui-hit ev) 1) (set! n (+ n 1)) (draw) (loop (ui-event)))
        (else (loop (ui-event)))))
```

Positions and sizes are in points: one pixel on most panels, two on the Tab5's HiDPI one, so a layout fits every board. (ui-size) is the window's content in points.

* (ui-open title), (ui-close), (ui-size) -> (w h) or #f. One window per board; opening it again focuses it and starts a new picture.
* (ui-begin) ... (ui-end): the picture, one call per widget, drawn in that order. (ui-end) is #f when no window is open or the picture did not fit (about 6 KB of widgets).
* Widgets (an id is a number above 0, which events name):
* (ui-label x y text [bold] [muted]), (ui-section title x y w), (ui-fill x y w h [gray]) (0 ink .. 255 white)
* (ui-button id label x y w h [active]), (ui-card id title subtitle x y w h [active])
* (ui-checkbox id label checked x y w h), (ui-toggle id label on x y w h)
* (ui-slider id value min max x y w h), (ui-progress permille x y w h)
* (ui-stepper dec-id inc-id label value x y w h): - and + are two ids
* (ui-segmented base-id labels selected x y w h), (ui-tabs base-id labels selected x y w h): part i is base-id + i
* (ui-list id items selected x y w h): a scrolling list; a pick names its row as the event's index
* (ui-textfield id x y w h): a one-line field whose text the window keeps -- (ui-text id), (ui-text-set! id text). It takes the keyboard when it has the focus (the on-screen keyboard on a touch panel without one); Cmd+C/X/V and a long press work as everywhere.
* (ui-dialog base-id title message buttons): modal, over the rest; button i is base-id + i
* (ui-disable [parts]): the next widget is dimmed (bit i its i-th part: a stepper's - is 1, its + 2)
* (ui-event [ms]) -> an event, or '() when none came within ms (absent: wait for one; 0: just look), on Ctrl-C, or once the window is closed and nothing is left to read. An event is a list (type id index value x y):
* press: a widget tapped, clicked, or activated with Enter or Space (index: a list's row, a segment)
* change: a slider moved (value), a text field edited; submit / cancel: Enter / Esc in a text field
* context: a long press or a right click (x y in points); tap: a tap on no widget
* key: a key no widget took (value the key: 113 is q; index the modifiers)
* resize: the window changed size -- draw again; close: it was closed
* (ui-hit ev) -> the widget's id for press, change, submit, cancel and context; #f otherwise.

Pressing, hovering, the keyboard's focus (Tab, Shift+Tab, arrows), a text field's editing and a list's scrolling happen in the window without the program. The shell's ui command shows the window and where each widget is; key and ms drive it from the serial console.

### Privileged Primitives
poke, net-config, net-identity (with a name: renaming), identity-name, identity-provision, identity-key, peers-add, peers-remove and wlan-set change keys, identity, network configuration or memory. They work from the console and from scripts. Evaluated for an ea tool call they refuse and return #f, and file writes under any system/etc/ directory fail there too (kernel/include/kernel/privilege.h).

## 5. Computer Algebra System (CAS) Scripting

To utilize symbolic mathematics in Lisp:

```lisp
lsh> lisp
(load "/sd0/cas/simplify.lisp")

;; Simplify expressions
(simplify '(+ (* 2 x) (* 3 x)))
;; => (* 5 x)

;; Symbolic differentiation
(diff '(* x (sin x)) 'x)
;; => (+ (sin x) (* x (cos x)))

;; Exact polynomial roots
(poly-factor '(+ (^ x 2) (* 5 x) 6) 'x)
;; => (* (+ x 2) (+ x 3))
```
For complete CAS documentation, see the CAS Manual.

